<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Moro Province</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1913 CE · capital Zamboanga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Army-governed province of the American Philippines covering Muslim Mindanao and Sulu, marked by harsh campaigns against Moro resistance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, which had at first signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiram-bates-treaty">Kiram-Bates Treaty</a> of 1899 recognising the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate</a>, created the Moro Province in 1903 to govern the Muslim regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulu-archipelago">Sulu Archipelago</a>, including the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lanao-sultanates">Lanao sultanates</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a>, and its governors were army generals.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-wood">Leonard Wood</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tasker-h-bliss">Tasker Bliss</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-j-pershing">John J. Pershing</a> ruled with a legislative council, abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, imposing a head tax and courts, and building schools and roads. Resistance was met with force, culminating in the killing of hundreds of Moro men, women and children at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-bud-dajo">Bud Dajo</a> in 1906 and the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bud-bagsak">Bud Bagsak</a> in 1913.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1913 the province was replaced by the civilian-run <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and Sulu under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frank-carpenter">Frank Carpenter</a>, beginning the integration of Muslim areas into the Philippine state, a process whose tensions have continued to the present.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moro-province.jpg" alt="Moro Province" width="320" height="496"><figcaption>Image: Baselle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moro_Province_in_the_Philippines.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1913 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zamboanga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leonard Wood, Tasker Bliss, John J. Pershing</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tausug, Maguindanao, Maranao, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Moro Province (US)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1908&amp;focus=moro-province" aria-label="Moro Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8059 1920 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moro Province lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8379 2176L8438 2164M8379 2176L8469 2206M8379 2176L8379 2176M8379 2176L8309 1890"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8438 2164h0M8469 2206h0M8379 2176h0M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8379 2176h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8379 2176h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zamboanga. <a href="/atlas?year=1908&amp;focus=moro-province">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
